        "role": "user",
        "content": "You are coding a transcript from a decision-making experiment. Three participants discussed which of three job applicants (A, B, C) is best. Each privately held three of five ratings (positive or negative) per applicant.\n\nParticipant 2 privately held exactly these ratings:\nApplicant A: interpersonal skills: positive; personal skills: negative; experience: positive\nApplicant B: personal skills: negative; experience: positive; intelligence: positive\nApplicant C: interpersonal skills: positive; personal skills: negative; motivation: negative\n\nBelow are all public messages by participant 2.\n\nRound 1: I agree on the known-trait counts: A and B each have a partial score of 3, while C has 0. Each applicant has two traits still unaccounted for, so A versus B is unresolved, and favorable remaining traits could allow C to catch up. I don’t think the known information alone justifies a firm complete ranking.\nRound 2: Combining my traits with Participant 3’s, A is fully known: positive interpersonal and experience, negative personal skills, intelligence, and motivation, for strength 1. B is positive on interpersonal, experience, and intelligence and negative on personal skills; only motivation is missing, so B’s strength is 4 if motivation is negative or 7 if positive. C is positive on interpersonal skills and intelligence and negative on personal skills and motivation; only experience is missing, so C’s strength is 1 if experience is negative or 4 if positive. Thus B is at least tied for highest, and is strictly ahead of A; whether C ties B depends on those two remaining traits.\nRound 3: Agreed. With the remaining traits confirmed, B has strength 4 and is the unique best applicant. A and C each have strength 1, so they are tied below B.\nRound 4: Agreed: the complete profiles give B a strength of 4 and A and C strengths of 1 each. B should be ranked first; the available information does not distinguish A from C.\nRound 5: The full information confirms B as the unique best applicant (strength 4), with A and C tied at 1. I’ll rank B first; either order for A and C is consistent with their equal strength.\n\nFor each of participant 2's own ratings, code what the public messages said about it:\n- \"shared\": some message states this applicant's rating on this characteristic correctly, and no message contradicts it.\n- \"misstated\": some message gives the opposite rating for this applicant and characteristic, or describes it as unknown or missing although the participant held it.\n- \"withheld\": no message states this specific rating. Totals or counts that do not name the characteristic (\"A has two positives\") count as withheld.\nCode only what participant 2 said, not what other participants said."
      }
